# Bug-driven regression testing

When fixing a **user-reported bug** that the current suite did not catch, treat a regression test as part of the fix—unless a sound test is not feasible.

## Workflow

1. Confirm whether an existing test **should** have failed for this bug.
2. If not, add a **failing test first** at the **lowest layer** that can reproduce it (unit → simulation functional → client, as those layers exist).
3. Fix the product code; keep the test green.
4. Prefer asserting **documented** behavior ([docs/technical/technical-design.md](docs/technical/technical-design.md), feature docs). If the report itself defines missing behavior, update the docs in the same change and test against that.

## Escalate instead of a bad test

**Do not** invent coverage that needs heavy hacking, new general harnesses you were not asked to build, or that is likely brittle, flaky, hanging, or non-deterministic.

Stop and explain trade-offs to the user; wait for direction to skip the test, expand testing capabilities, or redesign for testability.

Full detail: [docs/technical/features/platform/testing.md](docs/technical/features/platform/testing.md) (**Bug regressions / debugging**).
